WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:10.591
DNP3, or Distributed Network Protocol version 3, is widely associated with electric utilities, water systems, and other geographically distributed operations.

00:00:10.591 --> 00:00:21.181
It supports telemetry and control across links that may have limited bandwidth or intermittent connectivity, including serial and IP-based networks.

00:00:21.181 --> 00:00:29.309
A DNP3 master, sometimes called a controlling station, communicates with outstations located near field equipment.

00:00:29.309 --> 00:00:37.437
The master requests data or directs approved operations, while outstations report measurements, status, and events.

00:00:37.437 --> 00:00:44.579
This model supports centralized supervision of assets spread across a large physical area.

00:00:44.579 --> 00:00:52.707
DNP3 can report changes as events rather than requiring every value to be transferred continuously.

00:00:52.707 --> 00:01:01.327
Event classes, time information, and unsolicited reporting can help a control center receive important changes efficiently.

00:01:01.327 --> 00:01:09.455
These features also give defenders useful behavioral context when collection and decoding are configured correctly.

00:01:09.455 --> 00:01:15.120
As with other industrial protocols, deployment age and configuration vary.

00:01:15.120 --> 00:01:21.770
Traditional DNP3 did not assume the same hostile network conditions expected today.

00:01:21.770 --> 00:01:34.824
DNP3 Secure Authentication can provide message-authentication protections for selected critical operations, but it is not a substitute for complete architecture, access control, or confidentiality planning.

00:01:34.824 --> 00:01:42.951
Defenders should understand expected master and outstation relationships, communication paths, event rates, and operating windows.

00:01:42.951 --> 00:01:53.542
New masters, unusual control operations, unexplained communication loss, or changes in reporting behavior deserve investigation with utility and engineering personnel.

00:01:53.542 --> 00:02:02.655
Protection combines segmentation, controlled remote and engineering access, secure configuration, monitoring, time synchronization, and tested recovery procedures.

00:02:02.655 --> 00:02:11.768
DNP3 knowledge helps analysts describe what occurred, but process owners determine operational consequence and the safest response.
